    "lawCode": "Amd §537, Lab L; amd §697, Tax L; amd §20, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7196\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the labor law, the tax law and the social services\nlaw, in relation to jury pools for United States district courts\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo increase the number of qualified persons for potential inclusion in\njury pools for United States District Courts in New York State.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nFor the sole purpose of identifying prospective jurors and subject to\nthe same restrictions and requirements of confidentiality applicable\nto New York State courts, section 1 of the bill amends paragraph (b)\nof subdivision 3 of section 537 of the labor law by adding a new\nsub-paragraph (ii) to allow any United States District Court clerk or\njury commission in New York State access to lists of persons receiving\nunemployment insurance benefit.\n\nFor the sole purpose of identifying prospective jurors and subject to\nthe same restrictions and requirements of confidentiality applicable\nto New York State courts, section 2 of the bill amends paragraph 3 of\nsubsection (e) of section 697 of the tax law to allow any of United\nStates District Court clerk or jury commission in New York State\naccess to mailing lists of New York State tax filers.\n\nFor the sole purpose of identifying prospective jurors and subject to\nthe same restrictions and requirements of confidentiality applicable\nto New York State courts, section 3 of the bill amends subdivision 6\nof section 20 of the social services law to allow any United States\nDistrict Court clerk or jury commission in New York State access to\nobtain the names and addresses of persons applying for or receiving\naid to dependent children, Medicaid, or home relief.\n\nSection 4 provides that the act shall take effect immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThere are four United States District Court districts in New York\nState. Due to limitations in New York State law, the United States\nDistrict Courts are unable to access the broad base of potential\njurors available to litigants in New York State courts. The\nunfortunate result of these limitations is that United States District\nCourts have access to fewer persons potentially qualified to serve as\njurors. Moreover, the number and types of cases now filed in the\nDistrict Courts has increased.\n\nSubject to statutory requirements, New York State courts have access\nto the following information for the purpose of assembling lists of\nprospective jurors: (1) Registered voters, (2) licensed drivers, (3)\nNew York State tax filers, (4) New York State unemployment insurance\nrecipients, and (5) New York State recipients of public assistance.\n\nThe United States District Courts in New York State can access lists\nof registered voters and licensed drivers only. Under current state\nlaw, Commissioners of the New York State departments in possession of\nlists of unemployment insurance recipients, tax filers and public\nassistance benefits recipients are prohibited from releasing these\nlists except as expressly authorized by law. This bill would authorize\nthe Commissioners to release these lists to a United States District\nCourt in New York State upon the request of the Chief Judge of such\nDistrict Court and subject to the same conditions applicable to the\nrelease of such information to New York State courts.\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nSince this bill will essentially duplicate the process in use in New\nYork State courts, additional cost should be nominal.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
